WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — satiation (90s) Narrator Gabriel

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.135
First, every job has a finish line, and extra computer work after that point is waste.

2
00:00:06.635 --> 00:00:13.362
Second, cheaper computer work makes people start many more jobs, so total use can keep rising.

3
00:00:13.862 --> 00:00:20.694
Third, money prices for AI are falling fast, but the electricity it burns is never free.

4
00:00:21.194 --> 00:00:26.588
Once an accounting ledger balances or a bug patch passes its tests, running an AI

5
00:00:26.588 --> 00:00:32.249
loop longer adds zero value and can even undo working code. Confusing the finish line

6
00:00:32.249 --> 00:00:38.974
of one task with all tasks across an economy distorts both software pricing and power-grid forecasts.

7
00:00:39.474 --> 00:00:44.882
We split total computing demand into two distinct numbers: effort per job and total

8
00:00:44.882 --> 00:00:50.160
number of jobs. A written completion check puts a hard ceiling on each individual

9
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:56.350
chore, while plunging unit prices steadily pull millions of brand-new chores off the sidelines.

10
00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:02.318
Published industry series show the dollar cost of fixed AI benchmark performance falling

11
00:01:02.318 --> 00:01:08.160
roughly thirteenfold per year since twenty twenty-three. Yet moving data across silicon memory

12
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.939
chips, driving physical motors, and delivering trusted human clinical care never become free.

13
00:01:14.439 --> 00:01:21.315
Products that optimize time-on-screen or bill by raw word counts reward wasted retries past the finish line,

14
00:01:21.315 --> 00:01:26.155
whereas completion-gated systems stop immediately and save real electricity.
